\description{
Function to compute the Hodges-Lehmann estimator of location
in the one and two sample case following a clever fast algorithm
by John Monahan (1984).
}
\details{
The Hodges-Lehmann estimator is the median of the combined
data points and Walsh averages.

It is the same as the pseudo median returned as a by-product
of \code{\link[=wilcox.test]{wilcox.test()}}
(which however does not calculate correctly as soon as ties
are present).

Note that in the two-sample case the estimator for the
difference in location parameters does not estimate the
difference in medians (a common misconception) but rather
the median of the difference between a sample from x and
a sample from y.

\code{sides} names the side on which the finite bound lies:
\code{"left"} yields \eqn{[lci, \infty)}, \code{"right"} yields
\eqn{(-\infty, uci]}. The estimator is unbounded, so the open side is
reported as \eqn{\pm\infty}.

\strong{Missing values.} \code{NA} and \code{NaN} are treated alike. With
\code{na.rm = FALSE}, a missing value in \code{x} or \code{y} yields \code{NA} - a scalar,
or \code{c(est = NA, lci = NA, uci = NA)} when an interval was requested. With
\code{na.rm = TRUE} the missing values are removed from each sample
separately; \code{x} and \code{y} are independent samples, not pairs. An empty
sample, given or left over after the removal, yields \code{NA} as well.
Invalid arguments are an error even when data are missing.

\strong{Infinite values} are ordinary order statistics. \code{NaN} is returned
when the median is undefined: if a pair yields \code{Inf - Inf} (\code{-Inf} and
\code{Inf} both in \code{x} in the one-sample case, the same infinity in \code{x} and
\code{y} in the two-sample case), or if the two middle order statistics are
\code{-Inf} and \code{Inf}.

\strong{Exact interval.} With \code{method = "exact"} the interval inverts the
Wilcoxon signed-rank (one-sample) resp. rank-sum (two-sample) test
(Bauer 1972). Its bounds are Walsh averages resp. differences
\code{x[i] - y[j]}, selected by Monahan's partitioning instead of sorting all
\eqn{O(n^2)} values, so large samples are no problem.
\itemize{
\item Samples below 50 (both below 50 in the two-sample case) use
the exact null distribution: the classic one without ties, the
conditional (permutation) distribution given the midranks with ties.
\item Larger samples use the normal approximation with continuity and
tie correction.
}
The results agree with \code{wilcox.test(exact = TRUE, conf.int = TRUE)}
(R >= 4.6.0) in the two-sample case and in the one-sample case without
ties. With ties, \code{wilcox.test()} (R 4.6.1) takes the lower one-sample
bound from \eqn{P(V > v)} instead of \eqn{P(V \ge v)}, one rank too
narrow, with coverage below the nominal level; \code{hodgesLehmann()} uses
\eqn{P(V \ge v)}. In the large-sample case \code{wilcox.test()} interpolates
between Walsh averages with \code{\link[=uniroot]{uniroot()}}, \code{hodgesLehmann()} returns the
order statistics that bound the inverted test.
If the requested level is not attainable (very small samples, e.g.
\eqn{n \le 5} at 95\\%), the bound is infinite and a warning is issued.

\strong{Bootstrap interval.} If all bootstrap replicates coincide (e.g.
constant data), the interval collapses to \code{lci = uci = est}. With a
single observation, or when the replicates are not all finite, no
interval can be formed: \code{lci} and \code{uci} are \code{NA} with a warning.

\code{x} and \code{y} are not modified.
}
\note{
C++ port of Monahan's algorithm by Cyril Flurin Moser
